A||||3||btoth||Getting new engine installed, but I have 3.1 questions||||2||
Z||000000||btoth||04-18-2001||02:35 PM||||I am getting my engine replaced in my GT by a local shop that said they'd have no problem doing it.  The place I plan on ordering the engine from sells a higher-output 2.8 and the stock 2.8.  But what I really want to know is what's involved in the 3.1 conversion?  Can I just order a 3.1 long-block and have that installed, or would I have to go with a short-block and have the shop reuse the heads from the 2.8?<P>Any info would be appreciated. Thanks.||||reg||2
Z||000001||Voytek||04-18-2001||05:33 PM||||I just finished going through the same a couple of weeks ago.  What I ended up doing is getting my 2.8 bored out to 3.1 with 3.1 pistons and crank + performance cam.  I went thorough a lot of reasearch, though, prior to doing this.  This is what you need to keep in mind:<BR>#1. If you want a stock look and just want to switch the block (keeping the intake and EVERYTHING else from the Fiero) while keeping the job relatively simple, you MUST find a 3.1 with IRON heads, 60 degree engine (I think all the ones with iron heads are 60 degree).  This is the EASIEST way of doing this.  The aluminum heads ARE different.<BR>#2. If you don't care about stock look and slightly more work, you will get a bit more power with an aluminum head 3.1.  This will require keeping the 3.1 intake, etc.  Some say the exhaust manifolds won't fit on the aluminum heads: not true - I have checked my manifolds on an aluminum head engine and they fit just fine.  Keep this in mind: the aluminum head will probably have a coil pack.  This requires a computer switch-over and possibly a bracket to re-locate the coil pack (otherwise, not only will you never reach it by the firewall, I believe the front cooling line will protrude).  These aren't major mods but the results are quite good.  I have seen a Fiero with an aluminum head 3.1 - a VERY noticeable increase in power.<BR>#3: It's actually my first point - getting your 2.8 rebuilt into a 3.1.  The costliest of the 3 options but definitely the safest (you'd get a warranty with minimum mods).<BR>If I were to do this over again, I'd get the aluminum head 3.1 (I don't care about the stock look - my car doesn't look like stock anyways).<BR>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT GT 87||04-18-2001||07:33 PM||||All You Need is the 3.1 SHORT BLOCK  , rebuild your iron heads ( with S/S valves and port job) And reuse your intake and dist.  This is the cheap way. I might reccomend a balance job on the rotating stuff.  I have heard the alum. heads don't like the hot Fiero engine comp. The block has 4 bolts per cyl. not 5 like the s.b. chev, So alum. heads like to warp . I am building a balanced 3.1 .   You also need a neutrally ballanced fly wheel with the internally ballanced 3.1 crank................        Go for it!!!!!!!!!         Steve||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTZ34||04-18-2001||08:32 PM||||In addition to what doulble GT 87 said the shortblock should be built for a replacement engine with iron heads or you will have really (and I mean really) low compression.  ie. don't get a shortblock built for the splayed valve aluminum head engine.||||reg||3
